  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>110</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Hz</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>AC</mi></math> source is connected in the circuit (as shown in figure). The current through the resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>55</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>, at resonance in the circuit, will be _____<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/3e475d87-e3a6-4e4b-bcb9-539ed44954fe-image.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 171px;\" /></p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "0",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Let current in the inductance is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></math>,&nbsp;through the capacitor is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;and through resistance is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;at any instance as shown in the figure.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/556ecce3-c9ac-4b4c-b3f9-b01aa0fe7853-image.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 166px;\" /></p><p>According to the Kirchoff&#39;s junction law,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></math>.</p><p>At resonance&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></math>. Also, current through the capacitor and inductor have phase difference of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>rad</mi></math>. Therefore, they will form destructive interference and hence the current through the resistance will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
